<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Barisan Nasional</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1973">1973</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as National Front (Malaysia)</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Barisan Nasional (National Front) is a Malaysian coalition of parties formed in 1973 as the successor of the Alliance, led by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-malays-national-organisation">United Malays National Organisation</a> together with Chinese and Indian parties. It governed Malaysia continuously until it lost the 2018 general election after the 1MDB scandal. It later returned to government as a partner in wider coalitions.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/barisan-nasional.jpg" alt="Barisan Nasional" width="320" height="215" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alpaca Wikieditor · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Barisan_Nasional.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Barisan Nasional, or National Front, is the direct successor to the Alliance, a three-party coalition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-malays-national-organisation">United Malays National Organisation</a> (UMNO), the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaysian-chinese-association">Malaysian Chinese Association</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaysian-indian-congress">Malaysian Indian Congress</a>. It arose from the 1969 general election. The Alliance won a majority of seats but less than half the popular vote, losing ground to the newly formed Democratic Action Party and Gerakan and to the Pan-Malaysian Islamic Party. Tension between communities led to the riots of 13 May 1969 and a state of emergency.</p><p>After Parliament reconvened in 1971, negotiations began to widen the coalition. Gerakan and the People's Progressive Party joined the Alliance in 1972, followed by the Pan-Malaysian Islamic Party in 1973. The new coalition included regional parties from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabah">Sabah</a> and Sarawak and began with nine parties. It contested the 1974 general election under the prime minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdul-razak-hussein">Tun Abdul Razak</a> and won with considerable success.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The coalition kept the Alliance's inter-communal model on a larger scale and grew to include as many as 14 communal parties at its peak. In 1977 the Islamic Party was expelled after a revolt in the Kelantan state legislature. The coalition still won the 1978 election convincingly. It never took more than 67% of the popular vote between 1974 and 2008, but held a two-thirds majority of seats until 2008, helped by the first-past-the-post voting system.</p><p>In 2008 it lost more than one third of the seats to Pakatan Rakyat. In 2013 it won 47% of the popular vote but about 60% of the 222 seats. The 1MDB scandal, which broke in 2015, damaged its reputation. In 2018 it won only 79 seats and lost power to Pakatan Harapan, ending 61 years of rule counting its predecessor. Many component parties then left, and four remained: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-malays-national-organisation">UMNO</a>, MCA, MIC and PBRS.</p><p>After the Pakatan Harapan government collapsed in 2020, the coalition joined a Perikatan Nasional government. In the 2022 election it won 30 of 222 seats, behind Pakatan Harapan with 82 and Perikatan Nasional with 74. The result was a hung parliament, and the coalition supported Pakatan Harapan in a national unity government.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Each component party claims to represent one community and limits its membership accordingly, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-malays-national-organisation">UMNO</a> for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malays">Malays</a> and MCA for the Chinese. Membership of the coalition is mostly indirect, through its parties, though direct membership is allowed. It defines itself as a confederation of political parties. At elections all candidates stand under its symbol and there is a common manifesto, but each party also issues its own, and the parties compete among themselves for seats before nomination day.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Barisan Nasional” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>For more than six decades the country was governed by the same coalition, the Alliance and from 1973 the <b>Barisan Nasional</b>, led by UMNO.</blockquote><a href="/empire/malaysia">Malaysia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
